Currently the bulk of the Python functions are @b{static}.  And the
bulk of the non-static functions are only non-static to allow them to
be called from other files within the Python library.

There is one variable meant to be accessible outside the library --
@code{llpy_debug}.  And there are six functions -- @code{llpy_init}.
@code{llpy_python_terminate}, @code{llpy_register_script},
@code{llpy_execute_scripts}, @code{llpy_python_interactive},
@code{_py_traverse_nodes},and

@defun llpy_init (void) @arrow{}  void
Initialize the @code{Python} subsystem.

Normally the application does not need to call this function as both
@code{llpy_execute_scripts} and @code{llpy_python_interactive} call it.
@end defun

@defun llpy_python_terminate (void) @arrow{}  void
Terminate the @code{Python} subsystem.
@end defun

@defun llpy_register_script (char *script) @arrow{}  int
Register a @code{Python} script.
@end defun

@defun llpy_execute_scripts (int continue_on_failure) @arrow{}  int
Execute the registered @code{Python} scripts.  If
@code{continue_on_failure} is @code{true}, then if any script fails,
then the remaining scripts are still executed.  If it is @code{false},
then the remaining scripts are skipped.  Scripts are executed in the
order in which they were registered.
@end defun

@defun llpy_python_interactive (void) @arrow{}  int
Invoke the interactive @code{Python} interpreter.

An interactive @code{Python} interpreter is useful for a variety of
tasks including running quick tests and for resolving ambiguities in
the documentation.  For the latter, please report the issue so that
the documentation can be clarified.

For information on the @code{DeadEnds Python Extension Module}, how to
use it, and its internals, please consult the companion documents
@code{DeadEnds Python Extension Reference Manual} and @code{DeadEnds
Python Extension Internals Manual}.
@end defun

@defun _py_traverse_nodes (GNode* node, int level, int (*func)(GNode*, int, void*), void* extra) @arrow{} int
@b{This function badly needs a name change.}

This function is inspired by @code{traverseNodes} in
@code{Gedcom/gnode.c}, with three very imporatant differences:

@itemize @bullet
@item
We return success or failure, not void.
@item
There are three return values (not two) from the function called
on each node:
@*   --  1 success, continue normally
@*   --  0 success, done (early)
@*   -- -1 error, return failure
@item
The function called takes three arguments -- the node, the level,
and an opaque pointer -- instead of the two taken by traverseNodes.
The opaque pointer allows us to avoid communication via external
variables.
@end itemize

NOTE: while there are three return values from @code{func} recognized by
@code{_py_traverse_nodes}, depending upon @code{func}, there might only be two
meaningful return values for a particular call or use case --
e.g. if we are searching for a node, @code{retval=1} might well mean keep
searching and @code{retval=0} mean 'found it, recorded it in 'extra'.

Alternatively, if we are writing out nodes (e.g., as part of export),
then @code{retval=-1} might mean 'we encountered an error writing out the
node' and @code{retval=1} mean 'do the next node'.
@end defun
